<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ancient history</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3000 BCE–500 CE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ancient history is the period from the beginning of writing and recorded human history through late antiquity, covering all inhabited continents from about 3000 BCE to 500 CE. Recorded history, roughly 5,000 years long, begins with the development of Sumerian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> script. The three-age system divides the past into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, with recorded history usually counted from the Bronze Age, though the ages begin and end at different times in different regions. The world population is estimated to have risen from 45 million in 3000 BCE to 209 million by 500 CE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Ancient history covers all continents inhabited by humans in the period from about 3000 BCE to 500 CE. The three-age system divides it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, and the start and end of these ages vary between world regions. In many regions the Bronze Age is thought to begin a few centuries before 3000 BCE, while the end of the Iron Age ranges from the early first millennium BCE in some regions to the late first millennium CE in others. Before it lies prehistory, known mainly from the work of archaeologists. Homo sapiens left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> some 50,000 years ago, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> about 45,000 years ago and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> about 15,000 years ago.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a> is considered the cradle of civilisation. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, cities began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk-period">Uruk period</a> (4000–3100 BCE) and the surplus of storable food required record keeping and led to writing. Ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> began with the incipient unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-valley">Nile Valley</a> polities around 3100 BCE, traditionally under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menes">Menes</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a> developed around 3000 BCE, and by about 1600 BCE it had abandoned many cities, including Mohenjo-Daro, for reasons not known. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> the Shang dynasty is traditionally dated 1766 to 1122 BCE. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> the Minoan and Mycenaean civilisations gave way after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse">Late Bronze Age collapse</a> around 1200 BCE to the Greek Dark Ages and then the Archaic period, which lasted until the invasion by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> in 480 BCE.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, Andean civilisation began with organised fishing communities from 3500 BCE, and the Chavín culture emerged around 1000 BCE. Agriculture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> began around 8000 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> was grown from about 4000 BCE. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-son-culture">Đông Sơn culture</a> produced bronze drums weighing more than 70 kg by about 500 BCE, developed independently of Chinese or Indian influence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The world population is estimated to have risen from 45 million in 3000 BCE to 72 million by 1000 BCE and 209 million by 500 CE, a result of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic-revolution">Neolithic Revolution</a>. New philosophies and religions arose in both East and West, particularly around the 6th century BCE, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoism">Taoism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legalism">Legalism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> came to dominate thought, and in the West the Greek philosophical tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> was spread in the 4th century BCE by the conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. Early technologies include Egyptian ramps and levers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qanat">qanats</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>–Arabic numerals with zero from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and paper from China in the first century CE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ancient history” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
